# Pending human-applied patch — issue #679
#
# WHAT: adds a guard deny rule for raw-git-transport `git push` invocations whose effect can
#       reach the repository's default branch — refspec destinations naming it (bare name,
#       `HEAD:main`, `feature:main`, the `:main` delete form, `refs/heads/main`), `git push
#       --delete <remote> <default>`, `git push --mirror`/`--all` (either can carry the default
#       branch regardless of other args), and `--force`/`-f`/`--force-with-lease` combinations
#       (subsumed by the same refspec-destination check, not special-cased). Active ONLY when
#       the trusted spawn env `SAPWOOD_DEFAULT_BRANCH` is set (worker.ts wires it — see this
#       issue's ordinary-path PR); unset leaves the rule inactive, so non-worker `claude`
#       sessions are unaffected. Also adds guard.test.ts's BLOCK/ALLOW matrix for the rule
#       (below), which the applying human's own `npm test` (post-apply) exercises. Full design
#       rationale (bypass classes closed, accepted residuals) lives in docs/security.md's #679
#       section — not duplicated here.
#
# WHY IT IS A PATCH AND NOT A COMMIT: guard.ts / the guard hook wiring is human-merge-only
#       (CLAUDE.md "Non-negotiables"; canonical list docs/security.md "Human-merge-only paths").
#       An autonomous worker's write to guard.ts/guard-hook.ts is denied at the tool level:
#         BLOCK [write-path] guard/reviewer/merge-driver source is human-merge-only
#       (guard.test.ts itself is NOT on that list — a direct `guardDecision("Write", ...)` probe
#       ALLOWs it, same as any other ordinary test file — but its own hunk below only makes
#       sense alongside the guard.ts/guard-hook.ts hunks it tests, so it ships in the same patch
#       rather than as a separate normal commit.) The guard constrains Claude tool calls, never
#       a human's editor.
#
# APPLY (from the repo root), then delete BOTH this file AND the apply-check test that exists
# only to prove it in CI pre-apply (its job is done the moment the patch below lands for real —
# leaving it in place afterward would either dangle on a deleted patch file or, if made to
# tolerate that, silently stop verifying an accidental deletion of the real deny rule):
#       git apply docs/patches/679-guard-default-branch-push.patch
#       git rm docs/patches/679-guard-default-branch-push.patch
#       git rm engine/src/guard/patch-679-apply-check.test.ts
#       npm --workspace engine test    # exercises the new BLOCK/ALLOW rows this patch adds
